{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2188-03-25 21:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I’m Dr. Lee, one of the hospitalists. I’ll be helping take care of you tonight. Can you tell me what brought you in?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2188-03-25 21:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Hi, Doctor. I woke up at 4:30 this morning with terrible nausea and vomiting. I couldn’t keep anything down. My sister called my oncology clinic, and they sent me here."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2188-03-25 21:24:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. You mentioned you’re on chemotherapy. When did you start your last round?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2188-03-25 21:25:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I started Taxol just yesterday. This is my first dose. I’ve never had this kind of reaction before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2188-03-25 21:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That makes sense. Nausea and vomiting can happen after Taxol, especially if anti-nausea meds weren’t strong enough. In the ER, they gave you lorazepam and dexamethasone, and later fosaprepitant. That’s a strong anti-nausea combo."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2188-03-25 21:29:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I still feel sick, but not as bad as earlier. I just want to stop throwing up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2188-03-25 21:31:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll keep giving you IV fluids and anti-nausea meds. We also gave you IV morphine because you said you couldn’t swallow your pain pills. Your back pain is still there?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2188-03-25 21:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, it’s been there for weeks. I know it’s from the bone mets. But the nausea is new and much worse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2188-03-25 21:35:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Understood. We’ll manage both. Your labs show your white count is a bit high, but no sign of infection. Your liver enzymes are elevated, which we expect with liver mets."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2188-03-25 21:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is the chemo working? I’m scared it’s making things worse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2188-03-25 21:39:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The goal is to control the cancer. This nausea is a side effect, not a sign it’s not working. We’ll adjust your anti-nausea plan so future doses are easier."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2188-03-26 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. How are you feeling today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2188-03-26 08:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "A little better. I haven’t vomited since last night. Still queasy, but not as intense."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2188-03-26 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s progress. We’re going to try advancing your diet—start with clear liquids. If you keep that down, we’ll move to soft foods."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2188-03-26 08:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. I’m worried about taking my pills again. Will I get sick?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2188-03-26 08:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll give you ondansetron before you take anything by mouth. Also, your EKG yesterday showed a long QTc, so we avoided certain anti-nausea drugs. Today’s repeat EKG is better, so we can now add ondansetron safely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2188-03-26 08:25:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What does that mean, long QTc?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2188-03-26 08:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s a heart rhythm issue. Some anti-nausea drugs can make it worse, so we had to be careful. Now that it’s improved, we can use more effective meds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2188-03-26 14:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing well. You kept down apple juice and crackers. We’re moving you to a regular diet."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2188-03-26 14:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good. But I’ve started having a new pain in my chest. On the left side, when I twist or bend."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2188-03-26 14:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Let me check. Is it here? *presses on left chest wall*"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2188-03-26 14:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, exactly. It’s sharp when I move."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2188-03-26 14:37:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It seems like musculoskeletal pain—maybe from coughing or vomiting. But we’ll monitor. Your troponins are negative, so it’s not heart damage."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2188-03-26 14:39:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m glad it’s not my heart. But it hurts."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2188-03-26 14:41:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll treat it with heat and your regular pain meds. It should improve."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2188-03-27 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Morning. You slept okay? Any nausea?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2188-03-27 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No vomiting. Only mild nausea, and it goes away. The chest pain is better with the heating pad."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2188-03-27 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Great. Your labs this morning show your phosphorus is low. We’ll give you supplements for a couple days."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2188-03-27 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that from the vomiting?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2188-03-27 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, losing fluids and not eating can deplete electrolytes like phosphorus. It’s common in this situation and easy to fix."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2188-03-27 15:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We did a chest X-ray today because you had some shortness of breath this morning. It shows a bit of atelectasis—lung collapse from shallow breathing—but no pneumonia or fluid."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2188-03-27 15:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that serious?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2188-03-27 15:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not serious. It’s common when you’re not moving much or breathing deeply. We’ll encourage you to walk and do breathing exercises."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2188-03-28 07:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve made great progress. No nausea for over 24 hours, eating regular food, and your pain is stable. We’re planning to discharge you today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2188-03-28 07:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good news. What about my next chemo?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2188-03-28 07:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll need to call Dr. ___ before your next dose in three days. They’ll adjust your anti-nausea meds so this doesn’t happen again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2188-03-28 07:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I need IV meds every time?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2188-03-28 07:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No. With better prevention, you should tolerate it at home. We’re sending you home with ondansetron to take before chemo."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2188-03-28 07:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the phosphorus pills?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2188-03-28 07:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take them twice a day for two days. They’ll help your energy and prevent muscle weakness."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2188-03-28 07:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my back pain? It’s still there."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2188-03-28 07:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll continue your morphine and gabapentin. The pain is from the bone metastases, and we’re managing it as well as we can."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2188-03-28 07:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I was really scared last night."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2188-03-28 07:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I understand. You’re not alone. Your oncology team will keep working with you. We’ll get your discharge papers ready, and you can go home soon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2188-03-28 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just a final check before you leave. You’re alert, walking, eating, and nausea-free. All set."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2188-03-28 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I feel so much better than when I came in. Thank you."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2188-03-28 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Remember to call the oncology clinic before your next chemo. Take your meds, rest, and reach out if symptoms return."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2188-03-28 10:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. I’m glad I came in when I did."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2188-03-28 10:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Me too. You got the right care at the right time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2188-03-28 10:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll see my oncologist soon to talk about the long-term plan."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2188-03-28 10:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, they’ll discuss whether to continue Taxol or consider other options based on how you tolerate it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2188-03-28 10:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m willing to keep fighting, but I want to feel better."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2188-03-28 10:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s the goal—balancing treatment with quality of life. We’re all here to support you."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2188-03-28 10:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, Doctor. I really appreciate it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2188-03-28 10:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Take care, and don’t hesitate to call if anything changes."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2188-03-25 21:20:00",
    "admission_end": "2188-03-28 14:30:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with metastatic breast cancer involving the liver and bones was admitted for severe nausea and vomiting that began one day after starting Taxol chemotherapy. She was treated with IV fluids, dexamethasone, fosaprepitant, and lorazepam initially, with ondansetron added once her EKG showed improvement in QTc prolongation. Her symptoms gradually resolved, and she advanced to a regular diet without antiemetics. She developed transient chest wall pain, reproducible on palpation and not cardiac in origin, with a transient EKG change but negative troponins. Her back pain from known pathologic fractures remained stable on home opioids. Hypophosphatemia was noted and treated with oral supplements. She was discharged home with instructions to take phosphorus for two days and to contact her oncologist before her next chemotherapy dose to adjust anti-nausea prophylaxis.",
    "problems": [
      "Dehydration",
      "Secondary malignant neoplasm of bone",
      "Secondary malignant neoplasm of liver and intrahepatic bile duct",
      "Pathological fracture in neoplastic disease, other specified site, initial encounter for fracture",
      "Nausea with vomiting, unspecified",
      "Adverse effect of antineoplastic and immunosuppressive drugs, initial encounter",
      "Personal history of malignant neoplasm of breast",
